# Spiritual-Tech Super App — Engineering Rules (non-negotiable)

## Financial integrity
- Every balance deduction or financial ledger entry MUST use explicit PostgreSQL transactions
  (`BEGIN...COMMIT`) with row-level locks (`SELECT ... FOR UPDATE`). No read-modify-write on
  `Wallet.balance` outside a locked transaction.
- Never use floating-point numbers for currency; use numeric/decimal with 2 decimal places
  (`Decimal(12,2)` in Prisma, `decimal.Decimal` in Python, `Prisma.Decimal` in TypeScript).
  `number`/`float` for money is a review-blocking defect.
- Every wallet mutation MUST write a matching `WalletTransaction` row inside the same transaction,
  with `reference_type` and `reference_id` populated.
- `Wallet.balance` carries a `CHECK (balance >= 0)` constraint; code must pre-check solvency rather
  than relying on the constraint to fail.

## Ephemeris correctness
- Ephemeris calculations must strictly use Chitra Paksha (Lahiri) Ayanamsha (`SIDM_LAHIRI`) and
  True Node positions (`swe.TRUE_NODE`). Mean node and tropical output are forbidden.
- Always pass `swe.FLG_SIDEREAL | swe.FLG_SWIEPH | swe.FLG_SPEED` to `swe.calc_ut`.
- Ketu is always derived as the exact 180° opposite of the True Node, never computed independently.

## Real-time / RTC
- All WebSocket and RTC room events must validate user auth tokens and check real-time wallet
  solvency before admitting participants.
- LiveKit tokens are minted server-side only, are single-room scoped, and are never issued when
  `wallet.balance < astrologer.per_minute_rate * MIN_CALL_MINUTES_BUFFER`.
- Per-minute billing runs under a Redis (Redlock) distributed lock keyed by call session id; a tick
  must never execute concurrently on two workers.

## General
- TypeScript is `strict`; no `any`, no non-null assertions on external input. Validate all input
  with Zod (Node) or Pydantic (Python).
- No placeholder code, no `TODO` stubs in committed logic paths.
- Secrets come from environment variables only; never commit keys or `.env`.
